How it is computed

A level segment is a climb level-off when all of these hold:

it ends before the top of climb;
its altitude is at least 3,000 ft above the departure airport;
it starts within 200 NM of the departure airport;
it is not cruise: it is not both at or above 90% of the altitude of the top of climb and longer than 300 s.

A level segment is a descent level-off under the mirror conditions: it starts after the top of descent, its altitude is at least 1,800 ft above the destination airport (lower than in the climb, because an aircraft on final approach is close to level), it ends within 200 NM of the destination airport, and it is not cruise.
Each level-off gives a start event and an end event, with the altitude of the segment. Each takes the position of the report with a position nearest in time to it, within 60 s.

How it is computed

Take the reports of the pass that lie on the runway itself (between the two thresholds and within half its width of the centre line).
The pass is a crossing when all of these hold:

the aircraft’s median ground track is at least 45° away from the runway’s direction;
its ground speed never exceeds 40 kt;
the pass lasts at most 120 s;
the aircraft is on the ground at the start and the end: less than 15 ft above the ground, or flagged on the ground without an altitude (airliners send no altitude on the ground).

A pass on a runway that the same aircraft takes off from, lands on or turns off from in that pass is not a crossing of it. Only the take-offs and landings kept after the choice between parallel runways count here (see the runway-pass method): a reading on a parallel runway that was not kept does not remove a crossing.
runway-crossing-entry is the first report of the crossing and runway-crossing-vacated the last. Both are received reports.

A pass between 30° and 45° from the runway direction is neither a take-off or landing nor a crossing, and gives no event.

How it is computed
The detector works on the height of the aircraft above its destination airport, so it also finds approaches abandoned before the runway.

Find each moment the height falls through 500 ft and each moment it rises through 1,500 ft. A dead band of 50 ft on each side keeps an aircraft flying level near one of these heights from giving a series of crossings. The moments are interpolated between reports.
Pair each rise through 1,500 ft with the last fall through 500 ft before it. The time between them is one excursion.
Keep the excursion when all of these hold:

its lowest point is more than 15 ft above the airport, so the aircraft did not touch down (15 ft is the height that defines ALDT);
no report in between is flagged on the ground, and none is slower than 50 kt without an altitude (a landing roll or a taxi);
the aircraft climbs at 500 ft/min or more at some point;
the lowest point is at most 10 NM from the airport and lies in the approach corridor of one of its runways: at most 5 NM before a threshold or over the runway, and at most 0.5 NM from the extended centre line. When the airport’s runways are not known, this condition is not applied;
the excursion lasts at most 600 s, and no two consecutive reports in it are more than 60 s apart (a landing followed by a take-off, or a hole in the data, is not a go-around).

The event is placed at the lowest point of the excursion.

The data model
OPDI describes a flight with three kinds of information.
Flight. One row of the flight list per track. It gives the flight’s identifier (flight_id), the aircraft address and call sign, the date of flight, the departure and destination airports, data about the aircraft from the OpenSky aircraft database, and the main milestones of the flight, such as the take-off and landing times and runways. A milestone column is empty when the event behind it was not detected.
Event. A moment in a flight, such as the take-off, the top of climb or the crossing of a flight level. Every event has:

the flight it belongs to (flight_id);
a position: longitude and latitude in degrees, and an altitude in feet. The coordinates use WGS 84, the reference system of GPS. The position is empty for the block times;
a time (event_time, in UTC);
a type, for example top-of-climb or xing-fir;
a source, the origin of the position data (OSN for the OpenSky Network);
a version, which names the method that produced that event type. A method that changes gets a new version, so released data stay reproducible;
info: extra details as JSON text, for example the runway of a take-off or the two regions of a crossing from one flight information region (FIR) to the next. A FIR is an area of airspace in which one service provider gives information to flights.

Some things are not instants but periods, for example a level flight segment or a holding pattern. They are published as a pair of events, one at the start and one at the end (level-start and level-end, holding-start and holding-end).
Measurement. A number attached to an event, published as a column of the event’s row. Two are given for nearly every event: the distance flown since the start of the track (distance_flown_nm, in nautical miles) and the time passed since the track’s first_seen (time_passed_s, in seconds). Some event families add their own, for example the time spent in a holding pattern. Because the measurements are cumulative, subtracting the values of two events of one flight gives the distance and time between them.
Units follow aviation use: altitudes in feet (ft), distances in nautical miles (NM, 1 NM = 1,852 m), speeds in knots (kt, nautical miles per hour), vertical rates in feet per minute and times in UTC.
